00:55:29.706 --> 00:55:32.167
having the four hour work week experience.

1575
00:55:32.167 --> 00:55:33.651
or living in Bali,

1576
00:55:33.651 --> 00:55:36.444
and being digital, you know, kind of independent-

1577
00:55:36.444 --> 00:55:37.956
Digital nomad piece a part of this too

1578
00:55:37.956 --> 00:55:39.573
digital nomads and all of that.

1579
00:55:39.573 --> 00:55:41.483
And I think that's all super cool.

1580
00:55:41.483 --> 00:55:44.162
This is one step further to say, like,

1581
00:55:44.162 --> 00:55:45.846
"I don't want to do anything," essentially.

1582
00:55:45.846 --> 00:55:49.232
Right, so she also referenced a Twitter user

1583
00:55:49.232 --> 00:55:50.635
who posted, "I don't want a career,

1584
00:55:50.635 --> 00:55:52.916
I want to sit on the porch," and it got 400,000 likes.

1585
00:55:52.916 --> 00:55:53.749
And this person has 3,000.

1586
00:55:53.749 --> 00:55:56.491
I know. This is a woman who had like,

1587
00:55:56.491 --> 00:55:58.924
who has like 3,500 followers on Twitter.

1588
00:55:58.924 --> 00:56:00.634
It's not like a Twitter celebrity.

1589
00:56:00.634 --> 00:56:03.046
It's essentially like a fairly average person,

1590
00:56:03.046 --> 00:56:04.134
Right.

1591
00:56:04.134 --> 00:56:05.794
who tweeted this thing and it went crazy.

1592
00:56:05.794 --> 00:56:08.824
So certainly there's something afoot here.

1593
00:56:08.824 --> 00:56:10.185
How much of that is a reaction to

1594
00:56:10.185 --> 00:56:12.411
this current moment versus something

1595
00:56:12.411 --> 00:56:16.104
that's a little bit more generational or permanent?

1596
00:56:16.104 --> 00:56:18.403
I'm not sure we quite know right now, but.

1597
00:56:18.403 --> 00:56:20.104
But it's been going on for a while.

1598
00:56:20.104 --> 00:56:22.049
There's a book by Tom Hodgkinson called,

1599
00:56:22.049 --> 00:56:24.411
"How to be Idle," that had great reviews.

1600
00:56:24.411 --> 00:56:26.107
I found it through Dwight Garner,

1601
00:56:26.107 --> 00:56:27.504
who's my favorite literary critic.

1602
00:56:27.504 --> 00:56:29.769
I think I've mentioned him before on this podcast.

1603
00:56:29.769 --> 00:56:31.830
He's at the New York times and it's one

1604
00:56:31.830 --> 00:56:34.395
of his all time faves I think he wrote.

1605
00:56:34.395 --> 00:56:36.758
This book came out in 2005.

1606
00:56:36.758 --> 00:56:38.650
So this has been something some people have,

1607
00:56:38.650 --> 00:56:39.980
now he was kind of alone.

1608
00:56:39.980 --> 00:56:42.857
It kind of, the book is structured throughout a whole day.

1609
00:56:42.857 --> 00:56:44.739
Like 8:00 AM.

1610
00:56:44.739 --> 00:56:46.614
The first shot, I'll just read the first paragraph.

1611
00:56:46.614 --> 00:56:48.149
The first chapter is called,

1612
00:56:48.149 --> 00:56:51.245
"8:00 AM, waking up is hard to do"

1613
00:56:51.245 --> 00:56:53.286
And this is how it starts,

1614
00:56:53.286 --> 00:56:54.119
"I wonder if

1615
00:56:54.119 --> 00:56:55.840
that hardworking American rationalist and agent

1616
00:56:55.840 --> 00:56:57.874
of industry, Benjamin Franklin, knew

1617
00:56:57.874 --> 00:57:00.203
how much misery he would cause in the world.

1618
00:57:00.203 --> 00:57:03.901
When back in 1757, high on puritanical zeal,

1619
00:57:03.901 --> 00:57:06.838
he popularized and promoted the trite and patently untrue

1620
00:57:06.838 --> 00:57:09.709
aphorism 'early to bed and early to rise

1621
00:57:09.709 --> 00:57:12.370
makes a man healthy, wealthy and wise.'"

1622
00:57:12.370 --> 00:57:14.616
I've lived my whole life on that aphorism.

1623
00:57:14.616 --> 00:57:15.602
(Adam laughs)

1624
00:57:15.602 --> 00:57:16.518
Is it aphorism?

1625
00:57:16.518 --> 00:57:18.180
Am I using that word wrong?

1626
00:57:18.180 --> 00:57:20.676
No, I think I might've.

1627
00:57:20.676 --> 00:57:22.021
Yeah, like, okay, we're going

1628
00:57:22.021 --> 00:57:23.046
to have a revisionist history

1629
00:57:23.046 --> 00:57:24.878
on Benjamin Franklin and his approach

1630
00:57:24.878 --> 00:57:26.965
to life and work, like?

1631
00:57:26.965 --> 00:57:28.880
How dare Benjamin Franklin, you know,

1632
00:57:28.880 --> 00:57:32.631
basically pursue aspirational things.

1633
00:57:32.631 --> 00:57:33.478
Listen, now's not the time

1634
00:57:33.478 --> 00:57:34.929
to defend the founding fathers, Rich.

1635
00:57:34.929 --> 00:57:36.607
The childhood book

1636
00:57:36.607 --> 00:57:40.504
that I was given and read many times over

1637
00:57:40.504 --> 00:57:44.203
as a young person was, "The Early Bird Gets the Worm,"

1638
00:57:44.203 --> 00:57:47.196
and my whole life is all about the early bird gets the worm.

1639
00:57:47.196 --> 00:57:48.459
I've got a new life for you.

1640
00:57:48.459 --> 00:57:49.292
(indistinct)

1641
00:57:49.292 --> 00:57:51.107
"How to be Idle"?

1642
00:57:51.107 --> 00:57:54.450
"How to be Idle, A Loafers Manifesto,"

1643
00:57:54.450 --> 00:57:55.283
by Tom Hodgkinson.

1644
00:57:55.283 --> 00:57:58.783
Every instinct in me just balks at that.

1645
00:57:59.659 --> 00:58:01.345
You must read this then.

1646
00:58:01.345 --> 00:58:02.178
I know.

1647
00:58:02.178 --> 00:58:04.142
So here's the thing,

1648
00:58:04.142 --> 00:58:06.892
I think the healthy thing here is

1649
00:58:08.547 --> 00:58:10.903
is holding our relationship

1650
00:58:10.903 --> 00:58:13.561
to our work a little bit more lightly,

1651
00:58:13.561 --> 00:58:17.482
and understanding that who we are is not what we do.

1652
00:58:17.482 --> 00:58:20.482
And that's something that I struggle with

1653
00:58:20.482 --> 00:58:22.020
in an unhealthy way.

1654
00:58:22.020 --> 00:58:25.038
And I think probably most people of my generation,

1655
00:58:25.038 --> 00:58:26.766
you know, contend with.

1656
00:58:26.766 --> 00:58:29.276
I was recently, I'm not going to spoil it,

1657
00:58:29.276 --> 00:58:31.428
but I had a guest on the show

1658
00:58:31.428 --> 00:58:35.127
on an upcoming podcast that hasn't gone live yet,

1659
00:58:35.127 --> 00:58:37.366
who challenged me to take a year off.

1660
00:58:37.366 --> 00:58:38.199
A year!

1661
00:58:38.199 --> 00:58:39.118
At which I balked.

1662
00:58:39.118 --> 00:58:41.891
And they said, "Okay, how about six months?"

1663
00:58:41.891 --> 00:58:44.666
And it really made me anxious, in the moment.

1664
00:58:44.666 --> 00:58:47.871
Just being asked that question or being challenged

1665
00:58:47.871 --> 00:58:51.432
to do that because so much of who I am is tied up-

1666
00:58:51.432 --> 00:58:53.978
my identity is wrapped up in what I do.

1667
00:58:53.978 --> 00:58:55.913
And if I stopped doing that, what does

1668
00:58:55.913 --> 00:58:57.854
that mean about who I am, right?

1669
00:58:57.854 --> 00:59:00.331
And that goes into workaholism,

1670
00:59:00.331 --> 00:59:02.338
and how I think about myself,

1671
00:59:02.338 --> 00:59:05.083
and my attachment to relevancy

1672
00:59:05.083 --> 00:59:07.361
and you know, all of it, right?

1673
00:59:07.361 --> 00:59:08.489
And material things, right?

1674
00:59:08.489 --> 00:59:09.690
Material things, all of it like,

1675
00:59:09.690 --> 00:59:11.655
am I a provider? Am I a man?

1676
00:59:11.655 --> 00:59:14.321
Like, who am I, am I, you know,

1677
00:59:14.321 --> 00:59:17.427
meaningful if I'm not constantly putting content

1678
00:59:17.427 --> 00:59:19.559
out and all of it, right?

1679
00:59:19.559 --> 00:59:23.849
It challenges you to really deconstruct that

1680
00:59:23.849 --> 00:59:26.198
and evaluate that for yourself.

1681
00:59:26.198 --> 00:59:30.322
Which is scary for me, so I think within the context

1682
00:59:30.322 --> 00:59:33.369
of the conversation of work as a false idol,

1683
00:59:33.369 --> 00:59:36.443
there's important lessons to be mined about,

1684
00:59:36.443 --> 00:59:39.917
about, again, our relationship to work

1685
00:59:39.917 --> 00:59:42.334
in general and how we are all

1686
00:59:43.184 --> 00:59:45.411
in a culture in which identity

1687
00:59:45.411 --> 00:59:48.969
is so inextricably linked to what it is that we do.

1688
00:59:48.969 --> 00:59:49.802
I mean, it's a trope.

1689
00:59:49.802 --> 00:59:51.082
Like, you go to a party, what do you do?

1690
00:59:51.082 --> 00:59:53.213
And it's your career, right?

1691
00:59:53.213 --> 00:59:55.057
What does it mean if we reframe

1692
00:59:55.057 --> 00:59:56.805
that and ask a different question

1693
00:59:56.805 --> 01:00:00.828
or we let go altogether of attaching meaning

1694
01:00:00.828 --> 01:00:04.411
to work as a substrate for identity itself.

1695
01:00:05.524 --> 01:00:08.811
And I think as a thought experiment, if nothing else,

1696
01:00:08.811 --> 01:00:11.279
that's something important and healthy to do.

1697
01:00:11.279 --> 01:00:13.639
I'm not ready to, you know, be a loafer

1698
01:00:13.639 --> 01:00:16.709
and sit on the, you know, I want to do things in the world.

1699
01:00:16.709 --> 01:00:18.278
I have ambition and I'm not going

1700
01:00:18.278 --> 01:00:20.579
to feel guilty about that either.

1701
01:00:20.579 --> 01:00:22.947
But I do think having a healthy relationship with

1702
01:00:22.947 --> 01:00:24.619
that is important.

1703
01:00:24.619 --> 01:00:27.965
And I question how healthy my relationship

1704
01:00:27.965 --> 01:00:29.775
with that is right now.

1705
01:00:29.775 --> 01:00:31.508
It's healthier than it's been in the past,

1706
01:00:31.508 --> 01:00:34.300
but I think that there's, you know, more work

1707
01:00:34.300 --> 01:00:36.675
that I personally could do around this.

1708
01:00:36.675 --> 01:00:41.675
And I think that's why this piece like resonated with me

1709
01:00:41.796 --> 01:00:43.403
because it made me angry, you know?

1710
01:00:43.403 --> 01:00:45.069
Oh, really?
Yeah. A little bit.

1711
01:00:45.069 --> 01:00:45.902
Nice!

1712
01:00:45.902 --> 01:00:47.153
It's like, come on, like really?

1713
01:00:47.153 --> 01:00:47.986
Okay.

1714
01:00:47.986 --> 01:00:49.038
But she's such a great writer.

1715
01:00:49.038 --> 01:00:50.892
We're all gonna pat each other on the back

1716
01:00:50.892 --> 01:00:53.472
because we just want to lie down and we don't want

1717
01:00:53.472 --> 01:00:54.305
to go to work?

1718
01:00:54.305 --> 01:00:57.152
Well, I find it, like, it's an act of rebellion.

1719
01:00:57.152 --> 01:00:59.217
So it's like basically saying, "here's this,"

1720
01:00:59.217 --> 01:01:01.858
You know? Like do with what you want. She doesn't care.

1721
01:01:01.858 --> 01:01:03.352
She's on her parents porch.

1722
01:01:03.352 --> 01:01:04.486
But she is on her parent's porch.

1723
01:01:04.486 --> 01:01:05.345
Yeah, so parent's porch.

1724
01:01:05.345 --> 01:01:07.171
Right. Get your own damn porch.

1725
01:01:07.171 --> 01:01:08.004
Yeah, it's fine.

1726
01:01:08.004 --> 01:01:09.847
Her parents have to go to work and do that

1727
01:01:09.847 --> 01:01:11.025
so that she can lie down?

1728
01:01:11.025 --> 01:01:12.899
(Adam laughs)

1729
01:01:12.899 --> 01:01:14.097
I'm sure, you know, look.

1730
01:01:14.097 --> 01:01:14.930
Don't you think her-

1731
01:01:14.930 --> 01:01:17.447
What would Goggins say about all of this?

1732
01:01:17.447 --> 01:01:19.576
He would not be on team lie down.

1733
01:01:19.576 --> 01:01:20.593
(Rich laughs)

1734
01:01:20.593 --> 01:01:22.259
Right.
But I will say this,

1735
01:01:22.259 --> 01:01:25.426
So, if you look at the gradations of like American life

1736
01:01:25.426 --> 01:01:26.759
and work, right.

1737
01:01:27.697 --> 01:01:29.803
From the farm to the factory,

1738
01:01:29.803 --> 01:01:31.606
from the factory to growing, you know,

1739
01:01:31.606 --> 01:01:33.327
like the thing was to get into college,

1740
01:01:33.327 --> 01:01:35.043
and to work your way to the point where you don't

1741
01:01:35.043 --> 01:01:37.586
have to be working with your hands.

1742
01:01:37.586 --> 01:01:38.630
This was kind of like,

1743
01:01:38.630 --> 01:01:40.126
this is the mainstream culture, right?

1744
01:01:40.126 --> 01:01:41.771
I'm not saying I endorse any of this,

1745
01:01:41.771 --> 01:01:43.722
but this is how it kind of went.

1746
01:01:43.722 --> 01:01:45.148
And so then it became go to college

1747
01:01:45.148 --> 01:01:47.681
so you don't have to do what I did, son or daughter.

1748
01:01:47.681 --> 01:01:51.047
And you can look after yourself and have more, right?

1749
01:01:51.047 --> 01:01:52.833
It's all about have more, have more.

1750
01:01:52.833 --> 01:01:54.172
Then our generation came along,

1751
01:01:54.172 --> 01:01:58.812
and we saw that our parents who did these jobs,

1752
01:01:58.812 --> 01:02:01.787
if your parents are in the professional world doing like,

1753
01:02:01.787 --> 01:02:04.470
you know, teachers might find meaning,

1754
01:02:04.470 --> 01:02:05.784
but often lawyers don't.

1755
01:02:05.784 --> 01:02:07.009
You've had that personal experience.

1756
01:02:07.009 --> 01:02:08.090
Right, giant swaths

1757
01:02:08.090 --> 01:02:10.959
of the population who did that responsible thing

1758
01:02:10.959 --> 01:02:12.384
so that they could have the stuff,

1759
01:02:12.384 --> 01:02:14.702
but ultimately are unfulfilled.

1760
01:02:14.702 --> 01:02:15.535
Right.

1761
01:02:15.535 --> 01:02:16.368
And the kids know that their parents are unfulfilled.

1762
01:02:16.368 --> 01:02:17.201
And so the kids are like-

1763
01:02:17.201 --> 01:02:18.756
So the next generation pursue meaning.

1764
01:02:18.756 --> 01:02:20.725
Do what you love. Pursue what you love.

1765
01:02:20.725 --> 01:02:22.411
So we pursue it are what we love,

1766
01:02:22.411 --> 01:02:24.699
but then that becomes our entire being, right?

1767
01:02:24.699 --> 01:02:26.901
So like, yes, I got to do this.

1768
01:02:26.901 --> 01:02:27.865
This is who I am.

1769
01:02:27.865 --> 01:02:30.601
It becomes once you start your own business

1770
01:02:30.601 --> 01:02:33.063
or get into media or do whatever,

1771
01:02:33.063 --> 01:02:34.949
it becomes such an all encompassing,

1772
01:02:34.949 --> 01:02:36.193
you've had to fight so hard

1773
01:02:36.193 --> 01:02:38.693
to get whatever rung you're on

1774
01:02:40.639 --> 01:02:42.919
that you don't want to give it up.

1775
01:02:42.919 --> 01:02:44.617
And I understand that completely.

1776
01:02:44.617 --> 01:02:46.833
And it does become a little bit of who you are.

1777
01:02:46.833 --> 01:02:49.063
This is the next evolution of that.

1778
01:02:49.063 --> 01:02:51.896
Asking why is that? So it's really

1779
01:02:52.955 --> 01:02:57.542
just the same thing we were reflecting on our parents.

1780
01:02:57.542 --> 01:02:58.902
This is another level of that.

1781
01:02:58.902 --> 01:03:00.780
This is saying "Why are you like that?"

1782
01:03:00.780 --> 01:03:03.546
Isn't it a function of a dearth of meaning

1783
01:03:03.546 --> 01:03:04.624
in the workplace?

1784
01:03:04.624 --> 01:03:06.756
Like if people found greater meaning and purpose

1785
01:03:06.756 --> 01:03:09.296
in what they pursue career-wise

1786
01:03:09.296 --> 01:03:12.669
then work would not be the intolerable thing

1787
01:03:12.669 --> 01:03:15.499
that is motivating this movement

1788
01:03:15.499 --> 01:03:17.106
of rest as resistance.

1789
01:03:17.106 --> 01:03:19.816
I would put it this way,

1790
01:03:19.816 --> 01:03:21.503
Why does anything really,

1791
01:03:21.503 --> 01:03:23.608
(Adam laughs)

1792
01:03:23.608 --> 01:03:24.441
I hate to say this.

1793
01:03:24.441 --> 01:03:26.774
Are you going to get, like, nihilistic

1794
01:03:26.774 --> 01:03:27.817
on the cynics.

1795
01:03:27.817 --> 01:03:29.875
Yes! I'm going to go full cynic.

1796
01:03:29.875 --> 01:03:30.708
Anti-cynic.

1797
01:03:30.708 --> 01:03:31.719
No, this is full cynic.

1798
01:03:31.719 --> 01:03:33.110
I'm going to go full cynic.

1799
01:03:33.110 --> 01:03:35.368
What does it really matter if, like,

1800
01:03:35.368 --> 01:03:37.012
even Elizabeth Gilbert talks about this

1801
01:03:37.012 --> 01:03:39.207
and Big Magic's like, "No, one's asked you

1802
01:03:39.207 --> 01:03:40.906
to write that book," basically.

1803
01:03:40.906 --> 01:03:43.756
So Casey is saying, "No one asks you to do that."

1804
01:03:43.756 --> 01:03:44.773
Like, you don't need it.

1805
01:03:44.773 --> 01:03:46.386
The world doesn't necessarily need it.

1806
01:03:46.386 --> 01:03:49.340
Can you find meaning and purpose and fulfillment

1807
01:03:49.340 --> 01:03:51.540
without attaching it to doing something?

1808
01:03:51.540 --> 01:03:53.059
Maybe the do nothing theory.

1809
01:03:53.059 --> 01:03:54.115
Just the being.

1810
01:03:54.115 --> 01:03:56.244
So the book that I wrote before-

1811
01:03:56.244 --> 01:03:57.651
There's a Buddhist piece to this.

1812
01:03:57.651 --> 01:03:58.484
Yes.

1813
01:03:58.484 --> 01:04:00.290
The book I wrote before "One Breath"

1814
01:04:00.290 --> 01:04:01.977
was called "Middle to Somewhere."

1815
01:04:01.977 --> 01:04:03.580
It was a novel. Unpublished.

1816
01:04:03.580 --> 01:04:04.730
It got me an agent,

1817
01:04:04.730 --> 01:04:06.304
but we didn't get ended up-

1818
01:04:06.304 --> 01:04:08.686
it was like one of those almost not quites.

1819
01:04:08.686 --> 01:04:10.932
Didn't get a publisher. Still like it.

1820
01:04:10.932 --> 01:04:14.667
But the main character basically inherits

1821
01:04:14.667 --> 01:04:17.331
some money and decides to do nothing.

1822
01:04:17.331 --> 01:04:18.869
It's the do nothing theory.

1823
01:04:18.869 --> 01:04:21.552
And he travels the world just to like try

1824
01:04:21.552 --> 01:04:22.897
to do as little as possible.

1825
01:04:22.897 --> 01:04:25.731
And the idea is that you,

1826
01:04:25.731 --> 01:04:28.253
by doing, the more you do,

1827
01:04:28.253 --> 01:04:29.337
the more you're impacting the world.

1828
01:04:29.337 --> 01:04:30.170
You know,

1829
01:04:30.170 --> 01:04:32.085
the sub question is,

1830
01:04:32.085 --> 01:04:33.752
or the sub thesis is

