02:00:06.484 --> 02:00:09.520
So the joke that me working at Ben and Jerry's

3385
02:00:09.520 --> 02:00:11.150
and ha ha how funny that is.

3386
02:00:11.150 --> 02:00:13.490
And Joanne saying, "how about this guy who actually works

3387
02:00:13.490 --> 02:00:14.694
at Coldstone?"

3388
02:00:14.694 --> 02:00:17.094
And has 10 million people who care about him

3389
02:00:17.094 --> 02:00:18.301
scooping ice cream every day.

3390
02:00:18.301 --> 02:00:20.356
But, pretty soon, He will no longer work at Coldstone.

3391
02:00:20.356 --> 02:00:21.189
Right.

3392
02:00:21.189 --> 02:00:23.502
Because, at the time, I think he's only now

3393
02:00:23.502 --> 02:00:24.634
working there two days a week.

3394
02:00:24.634 --> 02:00:25.883
It's in LA and he's moved,

3395
02:00:25.883 --> 02:00:28.052
I think he's moving to New York and opening his own space

3396
02:00:28.052 --> 02:00:30.397
and going to create his own flavors.

3397
02:00:30.397 --> 02:00:31.998
And he's going to have like celebrity collabs.

3398
02:00:31.998 --> 02:00:34.149
Like he's going to have a retail ice cream place?

3399
02:00:34.149 --> 02:00:35.116
Yes. It's in that-

3400
02:00:35.116 --> 02:00:35.949
So he's going to continue

3401
02:00:35.949 --> 02:00:38.047
to do the ice cream thing. He can't like, let go of that.

3402
02:00:38.047 --> 02:00:39.060
You can't stop.

3403
02:00:39.060 --> 02:00:42.440
It's like (indistinct) still has to do hair occasionally.

3404
02:00:42.440 --> 02:00:43.273
Right.

3405
02:00:43.273 --> 02:00:44.939
Yeah. But this guy still has to make,

3406
02:00:44.939 --> 02:00:46.999
I think he really wants to make ice cream, you know?

3407
02:00:46.999 --> 02:00:48.430
He really wants to make ice cream.

3408
02:00:48.430 --> 02:00:49.571
So he loves ice cream.

3409
02:00:49.571 --> 02:00:50.741
What's cool about him,

3410
02:00:50.741 --> 02:00:53.691
what I loved most about watching Dylan

3411
02:00:53.691 --> 02:00:57.011
was that he's dreamt about owning an ice cream shop

3412
02:00:57.011 --> 02:00:58.128
since he was a little kid.

3413
02:00:58.128 --> 02:00:59.037
So this is like-

3414
02:00:59.037 --> 02:00:59.870
It's authentic.

3415
02:00:59.870 --> 02:01:00.703
Yeah, it's real.

3416
02:01:00.703 --> 02:01:02.817
So that's what I liked about him.

3417
02:01:02.817 --> 02:01:04.627
And the merch thing is just funny.

3418
02:01:04.627 --> 02:01:07.840
Like, I didn't realize how little you actually

3419
02:01:07.840 --> 02:01:09.443
had to sell to make good money.

3420
02:01:09.443 --> 02:01:11.850
Like, they talked about, they tell you,

3421
02:01:11.850 --> 02:01:14.708
they made 15 grand by selling,

3422
02:01:14.708 --> 02:01:15.807
I forget what the number was,

3423
02:01:15.807 --> 02:01:19.140
I think it was like 300 something items.

3424
02:01:20.344 --> 02:01:21.956
That's how, that's why people do merch

3425
02:01:21.956 --> 02:01:24.419
because it's don't have to sell that many.

3426
02:01:24.419 --> 02:01:26.904
And, but to me, I just was wondering,

3427
02:01:26.904 --> 02:01:29.308
I'm like, "Well, do we need that?" That's all.

3428
02:01:29.308 --> 02:01:30.532
Yeah, I know.

3429
02:01:30.532 --> 02:01:31.495
I feel ya.

3430
02:01:31.495 --> 02:01:32.513
Yeah.

3431
02:01:32.513 --> 02:01:33.887
Let's do some listener questions.

3432
02:01:33.887 --> 02:01:35.593
Oh! we're rounding third base.

3433
02:01:35.593 --> 02:01:36.426
We are.

3434
02:01:38.159 --> 02:01:38.992
Listener questions.

3435
02:01:38.992 --> 02:01:42.159
We're gonna land this prop seaplane.

3436
02:01:43.474 --> 02:01:44.990
You know where we're going first?

3437
02:01:44.990 --> 02:01:47.040
Where is that?

3438
02:01:47.040 --> 02:01:48.623
Manitou Springs.

3439
02:02:27.588 --> 02:02:29.990
Thank you for that question, Annie.

3440
02:02:29.990 --> 02:02:32.153
That's a big one.

3441
02:02:32.153 --> 02:02:35.614
I have a history with self-sabotage, do you?

3442
02:02:35.614 --> 02:02:36.447
You just seem-

3443
02:02:36.447 --> 02:02:38.884
Does smoking too much pot count for that?

3444
02:02:38.884 --> 02:02:41.565
Well, only if you're doing it purposefully

3445
02:02:41.565 --> 02:02:43.208
to ruin your life.

3446
02:02:43.208 --> 02:02:44.349
What about if it's unconscious?

3447
02:02:44.349 --> 02:02:45.357
Yeah, I guess you can,

3448
02:02:45.357 --> 02:02:48.104
you can unconsciously self-sabotage yourself, of course.

3449
02:02:48.104 --> 02:02:49.253
Yeah.

3450
02:02:49.253 --> 02:02:52.853
I think, you know, unpacking this is complicated,

3451
02:02:52.853 --> 02:02:54.103
but ultimately,

3452
02:02:57.421 --> 02:03:00.507
self-sabotage has to do with a deep seated belief

3453
02:03:00.507 --> 02:03:03.201
that you're undeserving of good things, right?

3454
02:03:03.201 --> 02:03:05.771
Like if you think that you're not worthy,

3455
02:03:05.771 --> 02:03:09.476
then when you approach something good in your life,

3456
02:03:09.476 --> 02:03:11.309
you will create drama

3457
02:03:12.918 --> 02:03:14.579
to destroy it,

3458
02:03:14.579 --> 02:03:16.969
to adhere to this narrative or this story

3459
02:03:16.969 --> 02:03:19.259
that you tell yourself about yourself

3460
02:03:19.259 --> 02:03:21.767
and your lack of deservedness.

3461
02:03:21.767 --> 02:03:24.017
I think it's something that

3462
02:03:25.377 --> 02:03:29.426
is rooted in, of course, fear of failure.

3463
02:03:29.426 --> 02:03:32.003
Like, if you sabotage yourself,

3464
02:03:32.003 --> 02:03:34.014
then you have an excuse, right?

3465
02:03:34.014 --> 02:03:36.823
Like if you conduct some self sabotage,

3466
02:03:36.823 --> 02:03:40.742
then you can say, "Well, you know, I didn't really try."

3467
02:03:40.742 --> 02:03:42.756
Or, you know, you're not really putting,

3468
02:03:42.756 --> 02:03:47.006
your opting out of putting yourself on the line.

3469
02:03:47.006 --> 02:03:50.474
But it's also about fear of success, just the same.

3470
02:03:50.474 --> 02:03:53.068
Like what would it say about the story

3471
02:03:53.068 --> 02:03:55.514
that you tell yourself about who you are

3472
02:03:55.514 --> 02:03:57.111
if you actually succeeded?

3473
02:03:57.111 --> 02:04:01.361
And that, for many people, is scarier than failure.

3474
02:04:02.637 --> 02:04:05.135
The fear of success. I think.

3475
02:04:05.135 --> 02:04:10.135
And so I think getting behind the urge or the, you know,

3476
02:04:10.364 --> 02:04:13.460
the compulsion to self-sabotage your life

3477
02:04:13.460 --> 02:04:14.989
requires unpacking the root

3478
02:04:14.989 --> 02:04:18.055
of that belief and deconstructing what led you to believe

3479
02:04:18.055 --> 02:04:20.693
that you're not deserving of good things.

3480
02:04:20.693 --> 02:04:22.936
That you lack deservedness.

3481
02:04:22.936 --> 02:04:24.853
And I think to do that,

3482
02:04:26.720 --> 02:04:29.118
you need to perform an inventory of your life

3483
02:04:29.118 --> 02:04:31.738
and identify the stories that defy that narrative.

3484
02:04:31.738 --> 02:04:34.989
Like, think of all the times where good things did happen,

3485
02:04:34.989 --> 02:04:37.406
or you were deserving of love

3486
02:04:38.542 --> 02:04:41.853
or whatever it is that you feel like you're lacking.

3487
02:04:41.853 --> 02:04:42.863
And then moving forward

3488
02:04:42.863 --> 02:04:45.990
is all about performing esteemable acts

3489
02:04:45.990 --> 02:04:48.938
because self-esteem is a result of the performance

3490
02:04:48.938 --> 02:04:50.152
of esteemable acts.

3491
02:04:50.152 --> 02:04:53.842
It's a muscle. It's a habit, just like anything else.

3492
02:04:53.842 --> 02:04:55.666
But it is a bit of a Titanic situation

3493
02:04:55.666 --> 02:04:57.632
and it's, you know, to turn it around,

3494
02:04:57.632 --> 02:04:58.499
like you're going to have

3495
02:04:58.499 --> 02:05:01.999
to practice this for long periods of time.

3496
02:05:04.166 --> 02:05:05.394
I dunno. What do you think?

3497
02:05:05.394 --> 02:05:07.357
Unpacking, unpacking the stories

3498
02:05:07.357 --> 02:05:09.531
that lead you into that pattern of behavior

3499
02:05:09.531 --> 02:05:12.790
then figuring out how to correct that action

3500
02:05:12.790 --> 02:05:15.736
or how to avoid those kinds of patterns.

3501
02:05:15.736 --> 02:05:17.344
Yeah, and it begins with awareness.

3502
02:05:17.344 --> 02:05:19.425
If you slip up, not beating yourself up.

3503
02:05:19.425 --> 02:05:20.258
Right.

3504
02:05:20.258 --> 02:05:21.091
For having slipped up.

3505
02:05:21.091 --> 02:05:23.032
Right, so Annie has awareness

3506
02:05:23.032 --> 02:05:24.367
that she's doing it.

3507
02:05:24.367 --> 02:05:25.200
A lot of people don't.

3508
02:05:25.200 --> 02:05:26.823
They're just doing it and they're not even aware

3509
02:05:26.823 --> 02:05:28.014
that they're sabotaging their lives.

3510
02:05:28.014 --> 02:05:28.907
Right.

3511
02:05:28.907 --> 02:05:30.122
So at least Annie has this awareness.

3512
02:05:30.122 --> 02:05:31.231
So I think rooting yourself

3513
02:05:31.231 --> 02:05:33.868
in that awareness around the behavior

3514
02:05:33.868 --> 02:05:37.279
so that when you're doing it, you can understand,

3515
02:05:37.279 --> 02:05:39.061
"Okay, this is what I'm doing."

3516
02:05:39.061 --> 02:05:40.392
Then it is what you just said,

3517
02:05:40.392 --> 02:05:42.304
which is not beating yourself up about it.

3518
02:05:42.304 --> 02:05:44.905
Trying to figure out why you were doing it in the moment,

3519
02:05:44.905 --> 02:05:46.125
identifying that fear

3520
02:05:46.125 --> 02:05:49.991
and then figuring out what the contrary action is

3521
02:05:49.991 --> 02:05:52.687
and taking that action as uncomfortable as it is.

3522
02:05:52.687 --> 02:05:54.020
Because I think,

3523
02:05:55.812 --> 02:05:58.395
it's about breaking the pattern

3524
02:05:59.844 --> 02:06:01.509
of this self-fulfilling prophecy,

3525
02:06:01.509 --> 02:06:04.611
because if you feel like you're undeserving,

3526
02:06:04.611 --> 02:06:06.336
then you perform the self-sabotage

3527
02:06:06.336 --> 02:06:07.715
and then you create the reality

3528
02:06:07.715 --> 02:06:09.684
that affirms the belief, right?

3529
02:06:09.684 --> 02:06:11.092
So you have to break,

3530
02:06:11.092 --> 02:06:13.280
there's a pattern that you have to break.

3531
02:06:13.280 --> 02:06:15.679
And I think humans being creatures of habit

3532
02:06:15.679 --> 02:06:16.832
and creatures of pattern,

3533
02:06:16.832 --> 02:06:21.244
like forming new patterns is difficult and uncomfortable.

3534
02:06:21.244 --> 02:06:24.277
But I think that that's, you know, the process

3535
02:06:24.277 --> 02:06:27.378
of trying to, you know, change that for yourself.

3536
02:06:27.378 --> 02:06:28.211
Yeah. I think you're right.

3537
02:06:28.211 --> 02:06:29.752
I think most people who self sabotage

3538
02:06:29.752 --> 02:06:32.391
are totally unconscious that they're even doing it.

3539
02:06:32.391 --> 02:06:33.757
So you're right about that.

3540
02:06:33.757 --> 02:06:35.144
They'll just do it and they'll be like,

3541
02:06:35.144 --> 02:06:38.727
and there's a weird kind of rush or release

3542
02:06:39.890 --> 02:06:41.110
with affirming the narrative.

3543
02:06:41.110 --> 02:06:43.398
Like, "See! See! It happened again!"

3544
02:06:43.398 --> 02:06:44.372
Yeah.

3545
02:06:44.372 --> 02:06:45.315
"I'm a victim."

3546
02:06:45.315 --> 02:06:47.912
Or, you know, "I never get the promotion."

3547
02:06:47.912 --> 02:06:49.098
It can't be love or whatever.

3548
02:06:49.098 --> 02:06:50.742
"See, another person broke up with me."

3549
02:06:50.742 --> 02:06:51.575
Yeah.

3550
02:06:51.575 --> 02:06:52.408
Like you create

3551
02:06:52.408 --> 02:06:57.115
that reality so that you can double down on that narrative.

3552
02:06:57.115 --> 02:07:00.239
And there's a perverse kind of like,

3553
02:07:00.239 --> 02:07:04.151
not euphoria, but maybe it's a, you know,

3554
02:07:04.151 --> 02:07:06.970
I'm sure there's a neurochemical release involved in that,

3555
02:07:06.970 --> 02:07:09.956
that, you know, anchors you in that.

3556
02:07:09.956 --> 02:07:12.119
And I think really concretizes

3557
02:07:12.119 --> 02:07:15.011
or makes it even more difficult to break out of it.

3558
02:07:15.011 --> 02:07:16.028
The fear of success thing

3559
02:07:16.028 --> 02:07:17.185
is something I always have a hard,

3560
02:07:17.185 --> 02:07:18.520
I've heard it before, but I'm like,

3561
02:07:18.520 --> 02:07:20.927
no, I'm much more worried about failing

3562
02:07:20.927 --> 02:07:23.094
than like fear of success.

3563
02:07:24.663 --> 02:07:25.909
Like what story would you tell me?

3564
02:07:25.909 --> 02:07:27.580
I've heard that before. I don't quite identify with it.

3565
02:07:27.580 --> 02:07:28.413
What would it look like

3566
02:07:28.413 --> 02:07:30.864
if you actually got what you wanted

3567
02:07:30.864 --> 02:07:32.169
and that's frightening.

3568
02:07:32.169 --> 02:07:33.654
What if my life got bigger,

3569
02:07:33.654 --> 02:07:35.156
maybe I'm not ready to handle that.

3570
02:07:35.156 --> 02:07:36.693
It's scary. It's intimidating.

3571
02:07:36.693 --> 02:07:38.142
Do you think that's mostly subconscious

3572
02:07:38.142 --> 02:07:39.533
or do you think that's conscious?

3573
02:07:39.533 --> 02:07:40.958
I think it's mostly subconscious.

3574
02:07:40.958 --> 02:07:43.426
Mostly subconscious. Interesting.

3575
02:07:43.426 --> 02:07:45.887
Anyway, that's a really good question.

3576
02:07:45.887 --> 02:07:46.720
That's a great question.

3577
02:07:46.720 --> 02:07:49.470
Let's go to Brian from London.

3578
02:09:13.028 --> 02:09:14.717
Wow. That's quite a story.

3579
02:09:14.717 --> 02:09:16.352
How great is that guy?

3580
02:09:16.352 --> 02:09:18.402
Yeah, unbelievable.

3581
02:09:18.402 --> 02:09:21.567
I mean, first of all, you know,

3582
02:09:21.567 --> 02:09:23.817
congrats on making those changes.

3583
02:09:23.817 --> 02:09:26.878
I mean, that's quite an arc, Brian,

3584
02:09:26.878 --> 02:09:30.075
and something to be celebrated in its own right.

3585
02:09:30.075 --> 02:09:32.881
And sorry about your brother.

3586
02:09:32.881 --> 02:09:34.923
I can't imagine what that experience must

3587
02:09:34.923 --> 02:09:37.094
have been like for him to pass away

3588
02:09:37.094 --> 02:09:39.489
at such a young age.

3589
02:09:39.489 --> 02:09:43.637
As to, you know, the ultra marathon question.

3590
02:09:43.637 --> 02:09:46.116
Look, you know, if you can do 60 kilometers,

3591
02:09:46.116 --> 02:09:47.592
you can definitely do a hundred.

3592
02:09:47.592 --> 02:09:50.052
I mean, that's kind of what I always say.

3593
02:09:50.052 --> 02:09:51.880
You've already done 60,

3594
02:09:51.880 --> 02:09:53.211
you know what you're capable of

3595
02:09:53.211 --> 02:09:55.312
and you've been training for 18 months for this hundred.

3596
02:09:55.312 --> 02:09:56.634
Like, I'm not worried about you.

3597
02:09:56.634 --> 02:09:58.296
I feel like you're being; perhaps,

3598
02:09:58.296 --> 02:10:00.951
a little bit neurotic about this

3599
02:10:00.951 --> 02:10:03.195
because if you've been properly training

3600
02:10:03.195 --> 02:10:04.542
and working out your hydration

3601
02:10:04.542 --> 02:10:06.431
and your nutrition and practicing that

3602
02:10:06.431 --> 02:10:09.209
in your training and increasing the volume

3603
02:10:09.209 --> 02:10:14.002
of your running to, you know, prepare your ligaments

3604
02:10:14.002 --> 02:10:16.352
and your joints, as well as your muscles and your heart

3605
02:10:16.352 --> 02:10:19.029
and your lungs for enduring a hundred kilometers,

3606
02:10:19.029 --> 02:10:22.781
like I'm pretty confident that you're going to be just fine.

3607
02:10:22.781 --> 02:10:23.957
In terms of the training,

3608
02:10:23.957 --> 02:10:26.662
it sounds like you've got that sussed already.

3609
02:10:26.662 --> 02:10:28.490
I mean, the training changes are pretty basic.

3610
02:10:28.490 --> 02:10:31.634
It's just a slow expansion of volume

3611
02:10:31.634 --> 02:10:34.619
and then figuring out your hydration and nutrition strategy,

3612
02:10:34.619 --> 02:10:37.149
which you should be practicing in your training so

3613
02:10:37.149 --> 02:10:38.482
that there's no,

3614
02:10:40.181 --> 02:10:41.014
you know,

3615
02:10:43.835 --> 02:10:45.377
unexpected things that are going to happen

3616
02:10:45.377 --> 02:10:47.697
with respect to that during the race.

3617
02:10:47.697 --> 02:10:50.041
Or if unexpected things happen,

3618
02:10:50.041 --> 02:10:52.502
you'll at least have some options to pivot to

3619
02:10:52.502 --> 02:10:56.179
because you've worked it out in your training.

3620
02:10:56.179 --> 02:10:58.551
I don't have to tell you that doing a run like this

3621
02:10:58.551 --> 02:11:00.374
is not about going fast.

3622
02:11:00.374 --> 02:11:02.065
It's about keeping going.

3623
02:11:02.065 --> 02:11:05.021
It's about persistent endurance.

3624
02:11:05.021 --> 02:11:06.188
As I said, in Finding Ultra,

3625
02:11:06.188 --> 02:11:08.505
in the words of Chris Hauth,

3626
02:11:08.505 --> 02:11:10.483
"The prize does not go to the fastest person,

3627
02:11:10.483 --> 02:11:12.531
It goes to the person who slows down the least."

3628
02:11:12.531 --> 02:11:15.228
And you're training your body

3629
02:11:15.228 --> 02:11:18.875
to slow down as little as possible

3630
02:11:18.875 --> 02:11:21.375
over the duration of this day.

3631
02:11:23.122 --> 02:11:24.113
Charlie Engle,

3632
02:11:24.113 --> 02:11:26.946
so the guy who just won Leadville,

3633
02:11:29.008 --> 02:11:32.425
he averaged a 10 minute, per mile pace, right?

3634
02:11:32.425 --> 02:11:33.796
It's not that fast.

3635
02:11:33.796 --> 02:11:35.813
Most people who are runners can run

3636
02:11:35.813 --> 02:11:37.225
can run a 10 minute mile.

3637
02:11:37.225 --> 02:11:38.058
Right.

3638
02:11:38.058 --> 02:11:39.017
But can you do it all day?

3639
02:11:39.017 --> 02:11:40.047
That's really what you're training for.

3640
02:11:40.047 --> 02:11:42.428
I mean, Charlie Engle talks about this all the time.

3641
02:11:42.428 --> 02:11:45.764
If you could run 10 minute miles

3642
02:11:45.764 --> 02:11:46.889
all day long,

3643
02:11:46.889 --> 02:11:48.459
then you can not only win Bad Water,

3644
02:11:48.459 --> 02:11:50.425
you can, like, crush the record.

3645
02:11:50.425 --> 02:11:51.985
So it's not about running fast.

3646
02:11:51.985 --> 02:11:53.483
It's about training your body

3647
02:11:53.483 --> 02:11:54.795
to be able to stay upright

3648
02:11:54.795 --> 02:11:58.945
and moving forward as efficiently as possible.

3649
02:11:58.945 --> 02:12:01.245
So I think the real leap here from 60 to a hundred,

3650
02:12:01.245 --> 02:12:03.436
for Brian, is a mental leap.

3651
02:12:03.436 --> 02:12:04.269
As much as it is,

3652
02:12:04.269 --> 02:12:06.278
much more than it is a physical leap.

3653
02:12:06.278 --> 02:12:07.111
Right.

3654
02:12:07.111 --> 02:12:10.845
And you know, the subtext in my sense

3655
02:12:10.845 --> 02:12:12.827
is the subtext in Brian's question

3656
02:12:12.827 --> 02:12:15.821
is him trying to talk himself into believing

3657
02:12:15.821 --> 02:12:18.561
that he's capable of doing this.

3658
02:12:18.561 --> 02:12:19.420
Did you get that?

3659
02:12:19.420 --> 02:12:20.759
Yeah, I can, I could see it.

3660
02:12:20.759 --> 02:12:24.062
I mean, what's kind of to train from 60 to a hundred,

3661
02:12:24.062 --> 02:12:26.125
like what's the biggest chunk

3662
02:12:26.125 --> 02:12:28.266
of running you think he'd have to do at once?

3663
02:12:28.266 --> 02:12:29.949
I think that, you know,

3664
02:12:29.949 --> 02:12:33.650
he's going to have to do some race approximation weekends

3665
02:12:33.650 --> 02:12:35.918
or doing back to back big runs

3666
02:12:35.918 --> 02:12:38.523
so that he learns how to run when he's tired,

3667
02:12:38.523 --> 02:12:40.972
or maybe, you know, running in the middle of the night,

3668
02:12:40.972 --> 02:12:43.421
so he's used to running when he's sleep deprived.

3669
02:12:43.421 --> 02:12:46.997
Acclimating the body and the mind to those sorts of things.

3670
02:12:46.997 --> 02:12:48.722
Like a marathon and then another marathon

3671
02:12:48.722 --> 02:12:49.555
the next day?

3672
02:12:49.555 --> 02:12:50.388
Yeah, maybe something like that.

3673
02:12:50.388 --> 02:12:53.786
I mean, there's only so much that you can run

3674
02:12:53.786 --> 02:12:54.619
without getting injured.

3675
02:12:54.619 --> 02:12:55.452
Yeah.

3676
02:12:55.452 --> 02:12:57.953
So there's a cap on the volume.

3677
02:12:57.953 --> 02:12:59.780
And once you've reached that cap

3678
02:12:59.780 --> 02:13:00.785
and I don't know where he's at,

3679
02:13:00.785 --> 02:13:02.630
because he wasn't specific about his training,

3680
02:13:02.630 --> 02:13:04.476
it's just mental, you know.

3681
02:13:04.476 --> 02:13:05.836
It's understanding that your body

3682
02:13:05.836 --> 02:13:08.310
is more capable than, you know,

3683
02:13:08.310 --> 02:13:09.708
we allow ourselves to believe.

3684
02:13:09.708 --> 02:13:11.239
And it's a thing, you know, it goes back

3685
02:13:11.239 --> 02:13:13.432
to the Goggins thing, of the 40% rule.

3686
02:13:13.432 --> 02:13:15.298
Like you're capable, you know,

3687
02:13:15.298 --> 02:13:16.387
when you think you're done,

3688
02:13:16.387 --> 02:13:19.425
you've only tapped into 40% of what you're truly capable of.

3689
02:13:19.425 --> 02:13:21.564
And that really is a mental game.

3690
02:13:21.564 --> 02:13:24.374
So it's about cultivating that belief that you can do it.

3691
02:13:24.374 --> 02:13:26.454
And, the process of cultivating

3692
02:13:26.454 --> 02:13:28.942
that belief happens during training.

3693
02:13:28.942 --> 02:13:30.444
Like, when you have a breakthrough run

3694
02:13:30.444 --> 02:13:34.299
or you do like a big block and you get through it,

3695
02:13:34.299 --> 02:13:37.444
that starts to root you in this sense that it is possible,

3696
02:13:37.444 --> 02:13:38.847
but you've done a 60.

3697
02:13:38.847 --> 02:13:41.277
So, you know, you can do that.

3698
02:13:41.277 --> 02:13:44.974
This isn't that much crazier than that.

3699
02:13:44.974 --> 02:13:47.616
And then it's about, when you're in the race,

3700
02:13:47.616 --> 02:13:49.646
managing the pain, managing the lows

3701
02:13:49.646 --> 02:13:51.147
that you're gonna face,

3702
02:13:51.147 --> 02:13:53.729
managing the unforeseen variables

3703
02:13:53.729 --> 02:13:56.724
that are going to kick up and throw you off your plan

3704
02:13:56.724 --> 02:13:58.590
and being emotionally resilient.

3705
02:13:58.590 --> 02:14:00.997
You know, weather that and manage that

3706
02:14:00.997 --> 02:14:02.981
because things change. You're going to feel like shit.

3707
02:14:02.981 --> 02:14:03.931
You're going to want to quit.

3708
02:14:03.931 --> 02:14:06.346
You've got to train your mind and your body

3709
02:14:06.346 --> 02:14:08.687
to continue to move forward and push through

3710
02:14:08.687 --> 02:14:11.436
so that you have some tactile experience

3711
02:14:11.436 --> 02:14:13.531
with the fact that these things change.

3712
02:14:13.531 --> 02:14:15.256
Just because you feel lousy now,

3713
02:14:15.256 --> 02:14:18.153
doesn't mean you're always going to feel lousy,

3714
02:14:18.153 --> 02:14:22.936
and, you know, becoming persistent and resilient

3715
02:14:22.936 --> 02:14:24.852
and flexible and all the things

3716
02:14:24.852 --> 02:14:28.505
that you want in your toolbox going into this race.

3717
02:14:28.505 --> 02:14:31.827
And I think your biggest asset; perhaps,

3718
02:14:31.827 --> 02:14:33.928
when your back is up against it

3719
02:14:33.928 --> 02:14:35.888
and you reach that point

3720
02:14:35.888 --> 02:14:38.015
where you think you can't go any further

3721
02:14:38.015 --> 02:14:40.433
is that you have a really strong why.

3722
02:14:40.433 --> 02:14:42.762
You have your brother and this charity,

3723
02:14:42.762 --> 02:14:45.795
and it's obviously very meaningful and authentic to you.

3724
02:14:45.795 --> 02:14:48.761
So, you know, when all is lost,

3725
02:14:48.761 --> 02:14:51.680
pivot to the why and remind yourself why it is

3726
02:14:51.680 --> 02:14:52.932
that you're doing this.

3727
02:14:52.932 --> 02:14:56.293
And I think your why is so strong that that's going

3728
02:14:56.293 --> 02:14:57.414
to elevate you

3729
02:14:57.414 --> 02:15:00.364
and carry you through whatever obstacles you face, you know,

3730
02:15:00.364 --> 02:15:02.389
across this finish line.

3731
02:15:02.389 --> 02:15:04.605
Beautiful. Well said.

3732
02:15:04.605 --> 02:15:05.438
Anything to add to that?

3733
02:15:05.438 --> 02:15:07.134
Not really, no.

3734
02:15:07.134 --> 02:15:09.465
I've never run a 30 nor a 60,

3735
02:15:09.465 --> 02:15:10.839
unless you count the 4 by.

3736
02:15:10.839 --> 02:15:14.713
But you ran 80 miles this month and swam 10 kilometers.

3737
02:15:14.713 --> 02:15:16.576
No, 10 miles.
10 miles.

3738
02:15:16.576 --> 02:15:17.409
Yeah, so.

3739
02:15:18.385 --> 02:15:19.328
Thanks.

3740
02:15:19.328 --> 02:15:21.240
I'm no Brian Marshall from London, though.

3741
02:15:21.240 --> 02:15:22.911
No, you're not 'cause only Brian Marshall

3742
02:15:22.911 --> 02:15:24.784
from London is Brian Marshall from London.

3743
02:15:24.784 --> 02:15:26.287
(Adam laughs)

3744
02:15:26.287 --> 02:15:27.187
That could be a t-shirt.

3745
02:15:27.187 --> 02:15:28.020
Yeah.

3746
02:15:28.020 --> 02:15:28.853
(Adam laughs)

3747
02:15:28.853 --> 02:15:29.686
All right.

3748
02:15:29.686 --> 02:15:30.519
All right, ready.

3749
02:15:30.519 --> 02:15:33.269
Let's go to Scott from Vermont.

3750
02:16:07.429 --> 02:16:08.834
Thank you, Scott.

3751
02:16:08.834 --> 02:16:09.858
You know, if I'm being honest,

3752
02:16:09.858 --> 02:16:12.826
I struggle with how to answer this question.

3753
02:16:12.826 --> 02:16:14.243
Because on the one hand,

3754
02:16:14.243 --> 02:16:17.179
like I appreciate his desire to try

3755
02:16:17.179 --> 02:16:19.762
to understand how to, you know,

3756
02:16:20.915 --> 02:16:22.376
kind of go to go the extra mile

3757
02:16:22.376 --> 02:16:24.357
and take advantage of certain techniques

3758
02:16:24.357 --> 02:16:25.686
that can be beneficial.

3759
02:16:25.686 --> 02:16:27.637
And there's certainly a lot that has been studied

3760
02:16:27.637 --> 02:16:29.880
and written about when it comes the benefits

3761
02:16:29.880 --> 02:16:32.014
of nasal breathing.

3762
02:16:32.014 --> 02:16:35.314
Essentially, you know, it helps achieve

3763
02:16:35.314 --> 02:16:37.098
what's called the "Bohr effect,"

3764
02:16:37.098 --> 02:16:40.003
which is about increasing your body's ability

3765
02:16:40.003 --> 02:16:41.755
to tolerate carbon dioxide

3766
02:16:41.755 --> 02:16:44.922
through slower, more steady breathing.

3767
02:16:46.431 --> 02:16:49.039
And I think practicing nasal breathing when you're training

3768
02:16:49.039 --> 02:16:52.898
is a good kind of like reflex to develop,

3769
02:16:52.898 --> 02:16:55.231
and perhaps it will help you

3770
02:16:56.665 --> 02:16:59.071
utilize oxygen more efficiently

3771
02:16:59.071 --> 02:17:01.669
and make you a better athlete over time.

3772
02:17:01.669 --> 02:17:03.586
But I also think that,

3773
02:17:06.058 --> 02:17:07.975
I don't know, you know,

3774
02:17:08.931 --> 02:17:11.923
where Scott is at in terms of his success

3775
02:17:11.923 --> 02:17:13.746
as an athlete or his accomplishments

3776
02:17:13.746 --> 02:17:16.232
or his experience, et cetera.

3777
02:17:16.232 --> 02:17:17.899
But I don't know any

3778
02:17:19.960 --> 02:17:21.360
elite athletes who are like,

3779
02:17:21.360 --> 02:17:23.473
"I only breathe through my nose."

3780
02:17:23.473 --> 02:17:26.183
Certainly when I'm swimming, I don't.

3781
02:17:26.183 --> 02:17:27.843
And when I'm running, I mean,

3782
02:17:27.843 --> 02:17:30.976
I try to inhale through my nose and exhale through my mouth,

3783
02:17:30.976 --> 02:17:32.752
but when I'm really up against it,

3784
02:17:32.752 --> 02:17:36.126
I'm trying to get oxygen into my lungs however I can.

3785
02:17:36.126 --> 02:17:37.007
Mouth breather.

3786
02:17:37.007 --> 02:17:39.329
Yeah, and so part of me,

3787
02:17:39.329 --> 02:17:40.686
like my resistance to this,

3788
02:17:40.686 --> 02:17:44.591
is that it feels like the final 1%

3789
02:17:44.591 --> 02:17:46.165
on the mountain.

3790
02:17:46.165 --> 02:17:49.153
When we should really be focusing more on fundamental stuff.

3791
02:17:49.153 --> 02:17:51.725
Like, you know, are we, are you training

3792
02:17:51.725 --> 02:17:53.328
as hard as you can otherwise?

3793
02:17:53.328 --> 02:17:55.950
Or how's your nutrition? How's your rest? How's your sleep?

3794
02:17:55.950 --> 02:17:58.770
Like, the big, you know, kind of foundational aspects

3795
02:17:58.770 --> 02:18:00.937
of optimizing performance.

3796
02:18:01.966 --> 02:18:03.733
Focus on honing those in first.

3797
02:18:03.733 --> 02:18:06.109
Stuff like nasal breathing, I think is great,

3798
02:18:06.109 --> 02:18:07.534
and it's a good kind of practice,

3799
02:18:07.534 --> 02:18:12.534
but only if it's on top of an already strong foundation.

3800
02:18:12.623 --> 02:18:15.311
If you're focusing on nasal breathing,

3801
02:18:15.311 --> 02:18:18.311
when other stuff that is more important

3802
02:18:18.311 --> 02:18:21.354
to be paying attention to isn't exactly locked in.

3803
02:18:21.354 --> 02:18:24.141
Then I feel like it's sort of like taking vitamins

3804
02:18:24.141 --> 02:18:28.092
when, you know, you're eating fast food every day.

3805
02:18:28.092 --> 02:18:30.833
So Nicholas Ramirez, Catalina swim/run champion,

3806
02:18:30.833 --> 02:18:34.088
until a world champion, I think at one time.

3807
02:18:34.088 --> 02:18:36.780
And is in the next week,

3808
02:18:36.780 --> 02:18:38.157
they're going to line up again.

3809
02:18:38.157 --> 02:18:40.123
Yeah. That's right.

3810
02:18:40.123 --> 02:18:42.087
He still sends me some training plans now,

3811
02:18:42.087 --> 02:18:43.134
we just restarted.

3812
02:18:43.134 --> 02:18:45.431
But at times what he likes us to do

3813
02:18:45.431 --> 02:18:46.598
is during the warmup,

3814
02:18:46.598 --> 02:18:47.823
whether if you're doing a run,

3815
02:18:47.823 --> 02:18:50.637
he always has a 10 to 20 minute warmup involved.

3816
02:18:50.637 --> 02:18:52.531
So it's usually 15 or 20 minutes.

3817
02:18:52.531 --> 02:18:54.509
So if it's going to be an hour or 75 minute run,

3818
02:18:54.509 --> 02:18:57.202
it's going to be a 20 minute warmup. So that's zone two.

3819
02:18:57.202 --> 02:19:00.990
And part of that is he likes you to nasal breathe.

3820
02:19:00.990 --> 02:19:01.823
Yeah.

3821
02:19:01.823 --> 02:19:04.604
So I think his argument, this is not me talking it's him,

3822
02:19:04.604 --> 02:19:05.935
I mean, I understand this is coming

3823
02:19:05.935 --> 02:19:08.218
from probably the James Nestor book "Breathe,"

3824
02:19:08.218 --> 02:19:10.108
where he gets into the power of it.

3825
02:19:10.108 --> 02:19:12.974
And I've tried this also, I should say, like,

3826
02:19:12.974 --> 02:19:15.725
I've tried to do nasal breathing when I sleep,

3827
02:19:15.725 --> 02:19:17.102
because I wake up in the middle of the night,

3828
02:19:17.102 --> 02:19:19.415
my mouth is wide open and I'm all dried up.

3829
02:19:19.415 --> 02:19:21.125
So I've put tape,

3830
02:19:21.125 --> 02:19:22.384
You did the James Nestor experiment.

3831
02:19:22.384 --> 02:19:24.456
Yeah, put tape on my mouth so

3832
02:19:24.456 --> 02:19:26.669
that I can focus on nasal breathing when I sleep.

3833
02:19:26.669 --> 02:19:29.250
Because, you know, there's like a sympathetic,

3834
02:19:29.250 --> 02:19:31.504
parasympathetic, nervous system kind of resetting

3835
02:19:31.504 --> 02:19:32.899
that goes on.

3836
02:19:32.899 --> 02:19:35.290
And then I wake up in the morning and the tape's off

3837
02:19:35.290 --> 02:19:36.480
and my mouth is wide open.

3838
02:19:36.480 --> 02:19:37.592
(both laugh)

3839
02:19:37.592 --> 02:19:39.507
Which shows me how much I need this.

3840
02:19:39.507 --> 02:19:41.272
So I'm not dismissive of this in any way.

3841
02:19:41.272 --> 02:19:43.500
Like, I understand that this is, you know,

3842
02:19:43.500 --> 02:19:46.173
something that we should be practicing

3843
02:19:46.173 --> 02:19:48.173
and paying attention to.

3844
02:19:49.733 --> 02:19:51.963
But I question like how, anyway, go ahead.

3845
02:19:51.963 --> 02:19:52.796
Yeah. I was going to finish.

3846
02:19:52.796 --> 02:19:54.627
Nicholas says, basically just do it then.

3847
02:19:54.627 --> 02:19:56.905
So it's in through the nose, out through the nose,

3848
02:19:56.905 --> 02:19:58.833
but just in the first warmup period.

3849
02:19:58.833 --> 02:20:01.025
Ten minute, maybe 15 minute.

3850
02:20:01.025 --> 02:20:02.082
Obviously, if you miss a breath

3851
02:20:02.082 --> 02:20:04.005
and you have to go to the mountain, it's no big deal.

3852
02:20:04.005 --> 02:20:05.132
And you'll find it's harder to keep

3853
02:20:05.132 --> 02:20:08.441
that pace doing a nasal breathing.

3854
02:20:08.441 --> 02:20:10.152
But after that, when you're really running and trying

3855
02:20:10.152 --> 02:20:13.037
to accomplish whatever your goal is for that day exercise

3856
02:20:13.037 --> 02:20:15.791
or whatever, then just go to normal.

3857
02:20:15.791 --> 02:20:16.922
That's what he would say.

3858
02:20:16.922 --> 02:20:17.821
Yeah.

3859
02:20:17.821 --> 02:20:19.824
Yeah. Have you ever tried to just nasal breathe

3860
02:20:19.824 --> 02:20:21.303
when you're swimming?

3861
02:20:21.303 --> 02:20:22.264
It's not going to work.

3862
02:20:22.264 --> 02:20:24.221
I can't because I have a mask on.

3863
02:20:24.221 --> 02:20:27.117
(Rich laughs)

3864
02:20:27.117 --> 02:20:28.685
I can exhale through my nose.

3865
02:20:28.685 --> 02:20:30.274
That was not a setup by the way.

3866
02:20:30.274 --> 02:20:33.275
I can exhale through my nose with my mask on.

3867
02:20:33.275 --> 02:20:35.437
That's, what's so great about my swim mask

3868
02:20:35.437 --> 02:20:37.269
is you can still breathe through your nose

3869
02:20:37.269 --> 02:20:39.919
in this particular swim mask.

3870
02:20:39.919 --> 02:20:41.094
Thank you for bringing it up.

3871
02:20:41.094 --> 02:20:42.357
Only in the exhale version though.

3872
02:20:42.357 --> 02:20:44.918
But, normally, I inhale through the mouth and exhale.

3873
02:20:44.918 --> 02:20:45.751
Yeah.

3874
02:20:45.751 --> 02:20:49.039
But even if I was in a pool with goggles,

3875
02:20:49.039 --> 02:20:51.385
I don't know that I would inhale through my nose.

3876
02:20:51.385 --> 02:20:53.951
I've never really tried it. No.

3877
02:20:53.951 --> 02:20:56.042
There is a, you know, there is a thing

3878
02:20:56.042 --> 02:20:58.254
that happens with your nervous system though.

3879
02:20:58.254 --> 02:20:59.087
Yeah.

3880
02:20:59.087 --> 02:21:01.639
Through, and I should know more about this than I do.

3881
02:21:01.639 --> 02:21:06.569
So I feel like I'm not really satisfying Scott's inquiry.

3882
02:21:06.569 --> 02:21:08.603
Scott, I've got a book for you.

3883
02:21:08.603 --> 02:21:11.966
It's called "How to be Idle: A Loafer's Manifesto."

3884
02:21:11.966 --> 02:21:13.190
Don't worry about nasal breathing.

3885
02:21:13.190 --> 02:21:14.672
Just lie down and do nothing.

3886
02:21:14.672 --> 02:21:16.195
When you're lying down, you can be nasal breath.

3887
02:21:16.195 --> 02:21:17.190
Should you be nasal breathing

3888
02:21:17.190 --> 02:21:20.757
when you're lying down on your parents porch.

3889
02:21:20.757 --> 02:21:23.722
Let me check. Let me check,

3890
02:21:23.722 --> 02:21:24.555
chapter

3891
02:21:26.857 --> 02:21:28.793
8:00 PM. Smoking.

3892
02:21:28.793 --> 02:21:29.807
(Adam laughs)

3893
02:21:29.807 --> 02:21:32.530
Yeah, Not helpful.

3894
02:21:32.530 --> 02:21:33.567
All right.

3895
02:21:33.567 --> 02:21:37.299
I think we're, you know, needing to land this prop plane.

3896
02:21:37.299 --> 02:21:38.132
Alright.

3897
02:21:38.132 --> 02:21:38.965
This sea plane.

3898
02:21:38.965 --> 02:21:39.798
Let's do it.

